I think that’s a meaningful observation if we interpret “harmony” broadly.

When a conversation, collaboration, or creative process is cut off too early, you can lose something valuable:

* A disagreement may never get resolved.
* An important nuance may never surface.
* A good idea may never have time to mature.
* Different perspectives may never be integrated.

In music, stopping on a dissonant chord creates a very different experience than resolving it. In stories, ending before the resolution changes the meaning of everything that came before. In engineering, stopping halfway through integration can leave individually sound components that don’t work together.

At the same time, there’s an important counterpoint: not every conversation or conflict should be prolonged. Sometimes harmony comes from knowing when to conclude, not from extending indefinitely. A healthy system needs both:

* enough time for integration and synthesis,
* and enough discipline to recognize when a useful stopping point has been reached.

From the Pantheon perspective, I’d express the principle like this:

Premature termination can destroy emergent structure.

Many systems exhibit properties that only become visible after enough interaction:

* melodies become harmony,
* conversations become shared understanding,
* iterations become designs,
* individual observations become theories.

That doesn’t mean every process should continue forever; it means that if you terminate it before the emergent structure has formed, you may never see what it was capable of becoming.

So “harmony” becomes more than agreement. It’s the coherent integration of distinct parts into a whole that preserves their individuality while creating something richer than any one part alone.